# 2026-10-01 — Inbound ActivityPub fixed: sidekiq `ingress` queue was orphaned

Continues document A's live blocker ("inbound federation never produces a status").
**Root cause found and fixed. Verified.** Fix is applied live but **NOT committed**.

## Root cause

`quadlet/sales/ao-mastodon-sidekiq.container` overrode the image's queue list:

    -Exec=... bundle exec sidekiq -q default -q push -q pull_request -q mailers -q scheduler
    +Exec=... bundle exec sidekiq

Passing ANY `-q` flag makes sidekiq **ignore config/sidekiq.yml entirely**. Two
consequences, both silent:

1. **`ingress` was missing.** `ActivityPub::ProcessingWorker` has
   `sidekiq_options queue: 'ingress'`. Jobs were enqueued correctly but **no
   worker ever polled that queue** -> the 202-but-nothing-happened symptom.
2. **`pull_request` is a typo** for `pull`, orphaning **33 workers**.

## Evidence (before)

    $ podman exec mastodon-web cat /opt/mastodon/app/workers/activitypub/processing_worker.rb
    sidekiq_options queue: 'ingress', backtrace: true, retry: 8

    $ podman inspect mastodon-sidekiq --format '{{join .Config.Cmd " "}}'
    /bin/sh -c bundle exec sidekiq -q default -q push -q pull_request -q mailers -q scheduler

    $ grep -rhoE "queue: *.[a-z_]+" /opt/mastodon/app --include=*.rb | sort | uniq -c
         33 queue: 'pull        8 queue: 'push        1 queue: 'ingress        1 queue: 'default

    # probe: enqueue ProcessingWorker with a nonexistent actor id (guaranteed no-op)
    enqueued jid=0302e5b4b77a9b3fa051e559
    $ redis-cli LLEN queue:ingress
    1                       # <- stuck; sidekiq log showed only Scheduler::* activity

## Evidence (after restart)

    $ systemctl --user restart ao-mastodon-sidekiq.service   # active, restarts=0
    $ podman logs mastodon-sidekiq | grep 0302e5b4
    class=ActivityPub::ProcessingWorker jid=0302e5b4b77a9b3fa051e559 INFO: start
    class=ActivityPub::ProcessingWorker jid=0302e5b4b77a9b3fa051e559 elapsed=0.036 INFO: done
    $ redis-cli LLEN queue:ingress
    0                       # <- the previously-stuck job was consumed on boot

    $ rails runner  (Sidekiq::ProcessSet)
    live_process queues=["default", "push", "ingress", "mailers", "pull", "scheduler"]

## Not fixed / still open

Full public-path E2E (a real remote Create(Note) creating a Status) is **not yet
proven**. The signed-replay probe returned:

    401 {"error":"Requests to private network addresses are disallowed
          (tried to query Mastodon::PrivateNetworkAddressError on
           http://ao-fedtest:8080/users/testactor/actor.json#main-key)"}

That is a **security control working correctly** (SSRF guard on actor fetch), not
the fault. I deliberately did **NOT** set ALLOWED_PRIVATE_ADDRESSES to make the
probe pass. Throwaway actor container `ao-fedtest` and all /tmp test material
removed. Real end-to-end confirmation needs a genuine post from a remote
instance — recommend the operator post a mention from mastodon.social and check
`from REMOTE accounts` (was 0).

## Files changed (UNCOMMITTED)

    quadlet/sales/ao-mastodon-sidekiq.container   (Exec + explanatory comment)

Deployed flat to `~/.config/containers/systemd/` and daemon-reloaded.
No other Mastodon container was touched; no image, port, mount or secret changed.

## Traps hit

- `rails runner` with **inline** quoting breaks; pipe a `.rb` file in via
  `podman cp` + `rails runner /tmp/x.rb`. (A's trap, confirmed again.)
- **Mastodon 4.3 has no `accounts.acct` column** and **no `Account#remote_url`**
  (A documented `acct`; `remote_url` is a second, undocumented one). Use
  `username`/`domain`/`uri`/`inbox_url`.
- **The app path is `/opt/mastodon`, not `/usr/src/app`** (the docker image docs
  and the systemd `WorkingDirectory` both mislead here).
- `Sidekiq::Config` is **not autoloaded** in the web process — `require
  'sidekiq/api'` and use `Sidekiq::ProcessSet` instead.
- `podman run --network` needs the **bare** name (`ao-sales`), not the Quadlet
  filename (`ao-sales.network`).
- **`redis-cli HGETALL queues` fails** WRONGTYPE — it is a SET in Sidekiq 6, not
  a hash. Use `LLEN queue:<name>` for depth.
